const title       = 'Can Ravens Count? Numeracy and Number Sense in Ravens';
const description = 'Ravens show evidence of numerical abilities — distinguishing larger from smaller quantities, tracking number of cache events, and even demonstrating rudimentary symbolic number comprehension. This page examines what science has found about raven numeracy.';
const path        = '/questions/can-ravens-count/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',      url: '/' },
  { name: 'Questions', url: '/questions/' },
  { name: 'Can Ravens Count?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Can ravens count or do maths?', a: 'Ravens show numerical abilities: they can distinguish quantities (more from fewer food items), track the number of agents entering or leaving a situation, and in captive studies respond differentially to different numbers of objects. Whether this constitutes "counting" in a formal sense depends on definition. Ravens appear to have approximate number sense (distinguishing 1 from 3, 2 from 6) rather than precise sequential counting. No study has demonstrated ravens doing arithmetic (addition, subtraction), though some corvid research approaches this question.' },
  { q: 'Which birds are best at counting?', a: 'Among birds, corvids (ravens, crows, jackdaws) and parrots (particularly African Grey Parrots, studied extensively by Irene Pepperberg) show the strongest numerical abilities. Alex, an African Grey Parrot trained by Pepperberg, demonstrated labelling of small exact quantities up to 6, and could answer "how many?" questions correctly. Corvids in comparative tests show approximate number sense broadly comparable to primates. Pigeons have also been trained to respond to number, but show less flexible transfer to novel contexts than corvids.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens understand zero?', a: 'Understanding of zero — the concept of "none" as a quantity — has been demonstrated in some primates and recently in corvids. A 2021 study by Kirschhock, Ditz & Nieder in <em>Journal of Neuroscience</em> showed that crows could place "zero" (empty set) in its correct numerical position relative to other quantities (zero is less than 1, which is less than 2, etc.), a form of ordinal number understanding that extends to the empty set. As a close relative with similar cognitive abilities, ravens are expected to show similar capacity, though direct raven zero-concept testing is less published.' },
];
