    <h1>Can Ravens Count? Numeracy and Number Sense in Ravens</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens show approximate number sense — the ability to distinguish more from fewer and to track quantities. They can discriminate small quantities, monitor the number of cache events, and respond differentially to different object counts in experimental settings. Precise counting (tracking each unit in an ordered sequence) has not been definitively demonstrated, but ravens' numerical abilities are comparable to those of non-human primates and significantly exceed most non-corvid birds.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Approximate Number Sense</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Approximate number sense (ANS) — the ability to quickly estimate quantity without precise counting — is widespread among vertebrates, including birds. ANS allows discrimination when quantity ratios are large (e.g. 2 vs 10) and becomes less reliable as ratios approach 1:1 (e.g. 7 vs 8). Ravens show ANS in food choice situations: when choosing between two food amounts, ravens reliably choose the larger quantity when the ratio is 1:2 or greater, with performance declining as quantities approach equal size.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>In cache monitoring contexts, ravens appear to track the number of times they have cached food in a location — a form of numerical monitoring with direct survival relevance. Bugnyar's research group showed ravens recache food (move it to a new location) more after competitor ravens were nearby for multiple cache events than after single events, suggesting sensitivity to how many potentially compromising observations occurred.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Comparative Numeracy in Corvids</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Much of the most precise corvid numeracy research has been conducted with carrion crows and jackdaws rather than ravens, using controlled laboratory paradigms:<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Nieder's group at the University of Tübingen has extensively studied numerical cognition in carrion crows using neurophysiological recordings alongside behavioural tests. Crows can be trained to match quantities, order quantities, and respond to symbolic representations of numbers (abstract symbols associated with specific quantities).</li>
      <li>Single-unit recordings from crow pallium (the avian equivalent of cerebral cortex) show neurons that respond to specific numerical quantities — "number neurons" — analogous to those found in monkey prefrontal cortex during numerical tasks.</li>
      <li>Ravens, as the most cognitively capable corvid in many comparisons, are expected to show at least equivalent numerical abilities, though the neurophysiological work has focused more on crows.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Practical Numerical Monitoring</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Even without laboratory training, wild ravens appear to use numerical information in natural contexts:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Tracking observers:</strong> cache concealment decisions appear sensitive to the number of watching competitors, suggesting ravens count or estimate audience size.</li>
      <li><strong>Food division:</strong> at a carcass with multiple competitors, ravens appear to monitor relative amounts consumed, though this is difficult to separate from general quantity assessment.</li>
      <li><strong>Social counting:</strong> ravens can track the composition of coalitions — knowing when one's social allies are present vs absent in a group, which requires monitoring individual identities and overall group size.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

